164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Calorie expenditure refers to the total number of calories your body burns in a day, comprised of several components:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Basal Metabolic Rate \(BMR\): BMR represents the calories your body needs to maintain essential functions while at rest, such as breathing, circulation, and maintaining body temperature. It typically accounts for the largest portion of total calorie expenditure, often around 60-75%.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Exercise Activity Thermogenesis \(EAT\): EAT includes any structured exercise or movement you engage in, such as jogging, cycling, strength training, or playing sports. Physical activity can significantly increase calorie expenditure, depending on the duration, intensity, and type of activity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis \(NEAT\): NEAT encompasses the calories burned through activities other than formal exercise, such as walking, standing, fidgeting, household chores, and other daily movements. NEAT can vary widely between individuals and can have a significant impact on total calorie expenditure.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Thermic Effect of Food \(TEF\): TEF refers to the calories your body burns through digesting, absorbing, and processing food. Different macronutrients require varying amounts of energy to metabolize: protein typically has the highest thermic effect, followed by carbohydrates and fats.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Adaptive Thermogenesis: This component represents the calories burned in response to environmental factors, such as extreme temperatures or changes in altitude, as well as physiological factors like illness or hormonal fluctuations.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
