/T (Why is Sleep so Important?)
/E (Why is Sleep so Important?)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Sleep plays a crucial role in supporting fat loss through various physiological mechanisms. During sleep, the body undergoes important processes that regulate metabolism, appetite, and hormone levels. Insufficient sleep disrupts these processes, leading to imbalances in hormones like leptin and ghrelin, which control hunger and satiety. When sleep-deprived, levels of ghrelin, the hunger hormone, increase, while levels of leptin, the satiety hormone, decrease, leading to increased appetite and cravings for high-calorie foods. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Additionally, inadequate sleep can impair glucose metabolism and insulin sensitivity, which may promote fat storage and hinder fat loss efforts. Furthermore, sleep deprivation can lead to increased levels of stress hormones like cortisol, which can promote abdominal fat accumulation. Therefore, prioritizing adequate sleep duration and quality is essential for supporting fat loss goals and overall metabolic health.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Most adults need 7-9 hours of sleep per night for optimal health, well-being, and cognitive function.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 134 0 R
/Pg 22 0 R
/A [430 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Stress management is crucial for fat loss because chronic stress can significantly impact metabolism, appetite regulation, and fat storage. Elevated cortisol levels from stress can increase appetite, promote fat storage, and trigger emotional eating, leading to unhealthy food choices. Stress also disrupts sleep patterns, increases cravings for unhealthy foods, and undermines consistency in healthy behaviors like exercise and meal planning. By effectively managing stress, individuals can support their fat loss efforts by regulating hormones, reducing cravings, and maintaining consistent habits.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
